WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.760
A DDoS response should begin with confirmation and classification, not panic.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.340
The team should establish what is affected, what signals changed,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.840
whether users are impacted, and where pressure appears.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.180
Is the issue at bandwidth, edge, connection handling, application behavior, a dependency,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.760
or a combination?

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.780
The answer can evolve as evidence improves.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.840
Early uncertainty is normal, but responders should separate what is known,

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.780
what is suspected, and what is being checked.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.920
Escalation should happen quickly.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.540
Provider teams may need evidence, timestamps, affected services,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.920
source regions at a high level, traffic characteristics from their own dashboards,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.900
and the business impact.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:48.520
Internal teams need role-specific channels: incident command, network, cloud, application,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.140
SRE, security, support, communications, and leadership.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.080
The incident commander should keep the work organized so responders are not duplicating

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.780
efforts or making conflicting changes.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.760
Communication should be calm, factual, and scoped to the audience.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.180
Engineering channels need technical observations, actions, owners, and next checkpoints.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.960
Executives need impact, risk, customer effect, and decision points.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.700
Support teams need customer-facing language and known workarounds.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:24.360
Legal or compliance teams may need involvement depending on contractual or regulatory

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:25.700
obligations.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.120
Public status updates should avoid unsupported claims.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.220
A good update says what is impacted, what is being done, when the next update is expected,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.200
and what users can expect.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.060
Recovery is not simply waiting for traffic to fall.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.200
Emergency controls must be removed carefully because a control that helped during the

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.900
event may block legitimate users afterward.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:51.640
Check backlogs, queues, cache behavior, error rates, latency, user journeys, monitoring,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.420
provider tickets, and application health.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.680
Preserve logs, dashboards, tickets, and a timeline while they are still fresh.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.540
The post-incident review should improve runbooks, dashboards, provider contacts,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.320
architecture, controls, and ownership without turning the exercise into blame.
